<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah, Staff Engineer here at NAB. I've been with the team =
for about 8 years now, working primarily on our digital banking platform. T=
hanks for taking the time to chat with us today. Could you start by telling=
 me a bit about your background and what drew you to apply for this Senior =
Frontend Engineer role at NAB?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah, thank you for having me! I'm excited about this opportunity=
. I've been working as a Frontend Engineer for about 6 years now, with the =
last 3 years focused heavily on React ecosystem. Currently, I'm a Senior En=
gineer at a fintech startup where I lead the frontend architecture for our =
trading platform that handles about 50,000 daily active users.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What particularly draws me to NA=
B is the scale and complexity of challenges you face in digital banking. I'=
m passionate about building <strong>performant, accessible, and scalable us=
er interfaces</strong>, and I believe financial applications present some o=
f the most interesting frontend challenges - from real-time data visualizat=
ion to complex state management across multiple user flows. I'm also drawn =
to NAB's reputation for technical innovation and the opportunity to impact =
millions of customers' daily banking experiences.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's great to hear! I see you mentioned working with React ecosystem. At NA=
B, we use a fairly modern stack - React, Next.js, and we're currently evalu=
ating different state management solutions including Redux Toolkit, Zustand=
, and Jotai. Can you walk me through your experience with these technologie=
s and perhaps share your thoughts on when you'd choose one state management=
 solution over another?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely! I've had extensive experience with all of these technologies. Let m=
e break down my perspective:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Redux Toolkit</strong> h=
as been my go-to for large, complex applications. At my current company, we=
 use RTK with RTK Query for our trading platform because we need <strong>pr=
edictable state updates, time-travel debugging, and complex state derivatio=
ns</strong>. The DevTools integration is invaluable when debugging issues i=
n production. However, the boilerplate can be significant, and the learning=
 curve for junior developers is steep.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Zustand</strong> I've us=
ed in medium-sized projects where we needed something more lightweight but =
still powerful. It's particularly effective when you have <strong>cross-com=
ponent state that doesn't warrant the complexity of Redux</strong>. I imple=
mented Zustand for a dashboard project with about 15 components sharing sta=
te - the API is incredibly clean and the bundle size impact is minimal.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Jotai</strong> is fascin=
ating for <strong>bottom-up atomic state management</strong>. I used it in =
a recent project where we had a complex form with conditional fields and de=
pendencies. The atomic approach made it easy to compose state and avoid unn=
ecessary re-renders. It's particularly powerful when combined with React Su=
spense for async state.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For <strong>Next.js</strong>, I'=
ve leveraged both App Router and Pages Router. The App Router's server comp=
onents have been game-changing for performance, especially with financial d=
ata where SEO and initial load times are critical.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>My decision framework ty=
pically considers:</strong></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Team experience</strong=
>: Redux for teams familiar with Flux patterns</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Application complexity<=
/strong>: Jotai for forms and local state, Zustand for medium complexity, R=
edux for enterprise-scale</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance requirement=
s</strong>: Jotai's atomic updates for fine-grained reactivity</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Future scalability</str=
ong>: Redux if we anticipate significant growth in state complexity</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent breakdown! I can see you've thought deeply about the trade-offs. Le=
t's dive a bit deeper into performance optimization. In a banking applicati=
on, we often deal with real-time data updates - stock prices, account balan=
ces, transaction lists. How would you approach optimizing a React applicati=
on that needs to handle frequent real-time updates while maintaining smooth=
 user experience?</p>
